    // default Server Action body limit is 1 MB. Base64 inflates by ~4/3, so
    // anything over roughly 700 KB was rejected by the framework before our
    // handler ever ran — while both the composer
    // (components/customer/mail/Composer.tsx) and the server
    // (lib/actions-mail.ts MAX_ATTACHMENTS_TOTAL_BYTES) advertised 20 MB.
    // Customers attached a 2 MB PDF and the mail silently failed to send.
    // 20 MB of files encodes to ~26.7 MB; 30 MB leaves headroom for the rest of
    // the payload (recipients, subject, HTML body). lib/actions-mail.ts still
    // enforces the real 20 MB cap, with a message the customer can act on.
    serverActions: { bodySizeLimit: "30mb" },
    // Same class of bug as the serverActions limit above, a different layer:
    // middleware.ts's matcher covers virtually every path (including
    // /api/files/[serverId]'s upload route), and Next.js caps the request
    // body it will forward THROUGH middleware at 10MB by default —
    // independent of both nginx's client_max_body_size and the route
    // handler's own MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES check, which never even got a chance to
    // run: the body was already truncated to 10MB before either of those
    // layers saw it. Found live 2026-09-06 via a real production log line
    // ("Request body exceeded 10MB for /api/files/...") after nginx's size
    // limit and client_body_timeout were both already fixed and still didn't
